  "language_probability": 1,
  "text": "A transformer changes AC voltage for the power grid and your home. It transfers electrical energy between 2 coils without a direct wire connection. Alternating current enters the primary coil so its magnetic field continually changes. The iron core guides this changing magnetic field around a closed loop. The field then passes through the secondary coil on the other side. Because the field keeps changing, it induces an alternating voltage in this second coil. The number of wire turns controls the result. More secondary turns increase voltage, fewer secondary turns decrease it. So the transformer uses magnetic induction to change voltage while transferring power between its coils.",
